# CSS Learning Notes: Text Alignment, Inheritance, and Borders

## 1. CSS Text Alignment (`text-align`)

The `text-align` property is used to align text horizontally inside an element.

### Syntax

```css
selector {
    text-align: value;
}
```

### Common values

| Value | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `left` | Aligns text to the left |
| `right` | Aligns text to the right |
| `center` | Centers text |
| `justify` | Spreads text across the available width |

### Example

```css
p {
    text-align: center;
}
```

This centers the text inside the paragraph element.

The `text-align` property is inherited, so if it is applied to a parent element, its child elements can inherit the alignment.

---

## 2. CSS Inheritance

Inheritance is a CSS feature in which some properties applied to a parent element are passed down to its child elements.

For example:

```html
<div class="container">
    <h1>Hello World</h1>
    <p>This is a paragraph.</p>
</div>
```

```css
.container {
    text-align: center;
}
```

Both the heading and paragraph text become centered without writing separate CSS rules.

### Why does this work?

The `text-align` property is inherited. The child elements receive the alignment from their parent unless another rule overrides it.

Inheritance helps reduce repeated CSS code.

**Note:** Not every CSS property is inherited. For example, `margin`, `padding`, and `border` are not inherited by default.

---

## 3. `text-align` vs. `align-items`

These two properties are used for different purposes.

### `text-align`

Aligns text inside an element.

```css
.container {
    text-align: center;
}
```

### `align-items`

Aligns child items inside a Flexbox or Grid container along the cross axis.

```css
.container {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
}
```

### Differences

| `text-align` | `align-items` |
|---|---|
| Aligns text | Aligns child items |
| Does not require Flexbox | Commonly used with Flexbox and Grid |
| `center` centers text horizontally | `center` aligns items on the cross axis |
| Applied to a text container | Applied to a layout container |

### Understanding the cross axis

In Flexbox, the cross axis depends on the `flex-direction`.

- `flex-direction: row;` → cross axis is vertical.
- `flex-direction: column;` → cross axis is horizontal.

Example:

```css
.container {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
}
```

Here, the items are aligned vertically in the center.

