## 4. CSS Borders

The `border` property adds a border around an element.

### Syntax

```css
border: width style color;
```

Example:

```css
.card {
    border: 2px solid black;
}
```

This adds a 2px solid black border on all four sides of the element.

### Border styles

| Style | Description |
|---|---|
| `solid` | Continuous line |
| `dashed` | Broken lines |
| `dotted` | Dots |
| `double` | Two lines |
| `none` | No border |

---

## 5. `border-bottom`

The `border-bottom` property adds a border only to the bottom side of an element.

### Syntax

```css
border-bottom: width style color;
```

### Example

```css
.card {
    border-bottom: 3px solid black;
}
```

This creates a 3px solid black bottom border.

Other examples:

```css
border-bottom: 2px solid blue;
border-bottom: 1px dashed gray;
border-bottom: 4px dotted red;
```

### Difference between `border` and `border-bottom`

| `border` | `border-bottom` |
|---|---|
| Adds borders to all four sides | Adds a border only to the bottom |
| Applies to top, right, bottom, left | Applies only to the bottom |
| Example: `border: 2px solid black;` | Example: `border-bottom: 2px solid black;` |

If you want only the bottom border, you can use:

```css
.card {
    border: none;
    border-bottom: 2px solid black;
}
```

---

## 6. Troubleshooting CSS Borders

Sometimes a border may appear differently than expected because of another CSS rule.

### Example

```css
.card {
    border: 2px solid black;
    border-bottom: 3px solid red;
}
```

In this example:

- The black border appears on the top, left, and right.
- The bottom border becomes red and 3px thick.

If we want only a bottom border:

```css
.card {
    border: none;
    border-bottom: 3px solid red;
}
```

When a CSS property does not work as expected, check:

1. Whether the correct selector is being used.
2. Whether another CSS rule is overriding the property.
3. Whether the CSS file is correctly linked.
4. Whether the browser is displaying an older cached version.

